  "query_preview": "..Plenty of Monsoon moisture extends across northern\nArizona as the high pressure system as moved eastward into the\nsouthern plains and southerly flow and transported moisture\nnorthward. Right now a trough at 850 mb is supporting the showers\nand thunderstorms in northwestern Arizona, with showers and\nthunderstorms also developing along the rim and higher terrain \ndown to the White Mountains. The UA WRF model shows this active \npattern continuing through 6 PM MST/7PM MDT, and then decreasing \nas the potential area of development shifts southward. However, \ndue to the north-northeast storm motion, it is possible for rain \nto move into areas near and south of I-40 overnight and early \nThursday morning. The UA WRF model also shows and active monsoon \npattern for Thursday afternoon and evening, though the 12 and 15Z \nruns show a slight decrease in overall coverage. The Flash Flood \nWatch may need to be extended into Thursday if shower and \nthunderstorm coverage is still expected to be extensive enough to\nwarrant a watch.\n\nA mid-level trough is currently sitting off of the west coast and\nwill attempt to move inland. This could bring drier, mid-level\narea over northern Arizona and reduce shower and thunderstorm\nactivity across the region. However, if this trough fails to move\ninland or moves farther north, then the active monsoon pattern may\ncontinue into the weekend. Right now forecast trends does support\nthe trough moving inland and an overall decrease in monsoon \nactivity this weekend, but these trends will be monitored.",
